### Step 1: Define the Basic Structure

We'll create a `main` function that sets up the initial state of our calculator and handles the different modes. Here’s how we can define it:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Function prototypes
void displayMenu();
int getUserChoice();
double calculate(double num1, double num2);
void handleInteractiveMode(int choice, double num1, double num2);

int main() {
    int choice;

    // Initialize the calculator
    printf("Welcome to the Calculator!\n");
    displayMenu();

    while ((choice = getUserChoice()) != -1) {
        switch(choice) {
            case 1:
                handleInteractiveMode(1, 0, 0); // Example: Addition
                break;
            case 2:
                handleInteractiveMode(2, 0, 0); // Example: Subtraction
                break;
            case 3:
                handleInteractiveMode(3, 0, 0); // Example: Multiplication
                break;
            case 4:
                handleInteractiveMode(4, 0, 0); // Example: Division
                break;
            default:
                printf("Invalid choice.\n");
        }
    }

    return 0;
}

void displayMenu() {
    printf("\nCalculator Menu:\n");
    printf("1. Add\n");
    printf("2. Subtract\n");
    printf("3. Multiply\n");
    printf("4. Divide\n");
    printf("5. Exit\n");
}

int getUserChoice() {
    int choice;

    do {
        printf("Enter your choice (1-5): ");
        scanf("%d", &choice);
    } while (choice < 1 || choice > 5);

    return choice;
}
```

This code defines the basic structure of our calculator project. It includes a menu-driven interface that allows the user to choose between adding, subtracting, multiplying, dividing, or exiting the application. The `getUserChoice()` function ensures that the user enters a valid number within the range of options available.

